159: /**
160:  * A {@link SchedulerNG} implementation that uses the declarative resource management and
161:  * automatically adapts the parallelism in case not enough resource could be acquired to run at the
162:  * configured parallelism, as described in FLIP-160.
163:  *
164:  * <p>This scheduler only supports jobs with streaming semantics, i.e., all vertices are connected
165:  * via pipelined data-exchanges.
166:  *
167:  * <p>The implementation is spread over multiple {@link State} classes that control which RPCs are
168:  * allowed in a given state and what state transitions are possible (see the FLIP for an overview).
169:  * This class can thus be roughly split into 2 parts:
170:  *
171:  * <p>1) RPCs, which must forward the call to the state via {@link State#tryRun(Class,
172:  * ThrowingConsumer, String)} or {@link State#tryCall(Class, FunctionWithException, String)}.
173:  *
174:  * <p>2) Context methods, which are called by states, to either transition into another state or
175:  * access functionality of some component in the scheduler.
176:  */
177: public class AdaptiveScheduler
178:         implements SchedulerNG,
179:                 Created.Context,
180:                 WaitingForResources.Context,
181:                 CreatingExecutionGraph.Context,
182:                 Executing.Context,
183:                 Restarting.Context,
184:                 Failing.Context,
185:                 Finished.Context,
186:                 StopWithSavepoint.Context {
187: 
